#!/bin/sh
# SoftAP via the ESP32 (hostapd on the NG driver's cfg80211 AP mode + dnsmasq
# for leases; docs/wireless-esp-hosted.md P5). Enabled by default: /etc/ap-enabled
# ships in the image so a freshly flashed board is reachable over WiFi with no
# provisioning. `ap off` clears the flag. Mutually exclusive with STA mode
# (S43wifi) -- both share wlan0, and the `wifi`/`ap` switches clear each other.
#
# TRANSPORT COMES FROM `wifi up`, NOT FROM A LOCAL modprobe. This script used to
# modprobe esp32_spi itself and wait 10 s with no retry, which lost the race on a
# cold boot: the ESP needs 10-20 s, S44ap gave up and exited 0, then S45aa-stack's
# `wifi up` brought wlan0 back later and nothing ever started hostapd for that
# boot. `wifi up` owns the only retry loop (4 x 8 s, re-pulsing the ESP32 reset
# on PE4 each round), so use it. It is idempotent -- if wlan0 already exists it
# skips straight to bringing the link up -- which is why S45aa-stack calling it
# again a moment later is harmless.
#
# Backgrounded for the same reason S45aa-stack is: the retry loop can take ~40 s
# and rcS must not block on the radio.
[ "$1" != "start" ] || [ -f /etc/ap-enabled ] || exit 0
case "$1" in
start)
	(
		wifi up
		[ -d /sys/class/net/wlan0 ] || {
			echo "ap: no wlan0 after wifi up (ESP32 transport failed -- check wiring + dmesg)" > /dev/console
			exit 0
		}
		# Anchored: an unanchored match blocked startup whenever the token
		# appeared anywhere in the file, including in a comment explaining it.
		if grep -q '^wpa_passphrase=CHANGEME_AP_PASS' /etc/hostapd.conf 2>/dev/null; then
			echo "ap: edit /etc/hostapd.conf (placeholder passphrase), then: ap off; ap on" > /dev/console
			exit 0
		fi
		# NEVER `ip link set wlan0 down` here. On esp-hosted that calls ndo_stop,
		# which deinitialises the interface on the ESP32 side, and nothing brings
		# the firmware back -- cfg80211 and hostapd then both report an ENABLED AP
		# that never beacons.
		ip link set wlan0 up 2>/dev/null || ifconfig wlan0 up
		# -4: an unqualified flush also destroys the IPv6 link-local, and wireless
		# CarPlay hands the phone the fe80 address as its endpoint. (Measured: it
		# falls back to IPv4 and still works, so this is hygiene, not a fix.)
		ip -4 addr flush dev wlan0 2>/dev/null
		ip addr add 192.168.9.1/24 dev wlan0
		hostapd -B -P /var/run/hostapd.pid /etc/hostapd.conf || {
			echo "ap: hostapd failed to start (see dmesg / hostapd output)" > /dev/console
			exit 0
		}
		dnsmasq -C /etc/dnsmasq-ap.conf -x /var/run/dnsmasq-ap.pid
		echo "============ ap: SSID $(sed -n 's/^ssid=//p' /etc/hostapd.conf) up at 192.168.9.1 ============" > /dev/console
	) &
	;;
stop)
	for f in /var/run/hostapd.pid /var/run/dnsmasq-ap.pid; do
		[ -f "$f" ] && kill "$(cat "$f")" 2>/dev/null && rm -f "$f"
	done
	# -4 again, and note this deliberately leaves the transport loaded: taking
	# wlan0 away is `wifi off`'s job, not a side effect of stopping the AP.
	ip -4 addr flush dev wlan0 2>/dev/null
	;;
*)
	echo "usage: $0 {start|stop}"
	;;
esac
